# Mobile build-args for the Capacitor shells (iOS + Android).
#
# Copy this file to `.env.mobile` (gitignored) and fill in your REAL values.
# `.env.mobile` holds internal/prod URLs and must NEVER be committed.
#
# Consumed by `make mobile-build-internal` / `make mobile-build-release`, which
# source this file and export the vars before `npm run build && npx cap sync`.
# All `VITE_*` vars are baked into the JS bundle at build time (client-visible by
# design — DSN/website-id are public browser ids, not secrets); the URLs they
# point at are the only sensitive part, which is why the real file stays local.

# Prod GlitchTip/Sentry DSN — the PUBLIC ingest edge (a browser client can't reach
# the tailnet-only backend). Empty => no error reporting. `make mobile-build-release`
# FAILS if this is empty, so a shipped app can never silently lose crash reporting.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN_PLAYER=

# Umami analytics (cookieless). Both required to enable; empty => no-op.
#
# WHICH SITE ID GOES IN WHICH FILE (operator rule, 2026-10-03):
#
#   .env.mobile            -> the DEV site id. This file builds for YOUR OWN PHONE
#                             (`make ios-device-install`, `make android-build`), so its usage must
#                             not land in the cohort the beta is measuring.
#   .env.mobile.testflight -> the PROD site id. This file builds everything that goes to OTHER
#                             PEOPLE — `make ios-testflight`, `ios-testflight-release`,
#                             `android-bundle` (Play). Those testers ARE the beta.
#
# Nothing is hardcoded in the source any more: `services/analytics.ts` reads these and nothing
# else. The dev default used to be a literal website id that did not exist in Umami at all, so
# every dev event came back "Website not found." and dev analytics silently went nowhere.
VITE_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=
VITE_UMAMI_SRC=

# Absolute API base for native builds (web uses the origin-relative default).
# e.g. https://api.your-domain.example/api/app
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# The DEV tier's api base, used when the in-app tier switch is set to "dev".
#
# This used to be hardcoded in src/services/tier.ts as the developer's own tailnet host. That is a
# personal machine name: it tripped the operator identifier deny-list and baked a private hostname
# into every bundle, so it now lives here (this file is gitignored; only .example is committed).
#
# NORMALLY LEAVE THIS EMPTY. The build derives the tailnet base from the machine you build ON
# (vite.config.ts § resolveDevApiBase), so a physical device works with no configuration from
# either dev machine, and the simulator falls back to http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/app.
#
# Set it only when you build on one machine and serve the dev API from another:
#   e.g. VITE_DEV_API_BASE=https://<your-machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net/api/app
# `tailscale serve` fronts that host so iOS ATS gets a real HTTPS cert with no cleartext exception.
VITE_DEV_API_BASE=

# Coming-soon gate credential for the native PROD tier (internal builds only). While the site is
# pre-launch, closelistening.app gates /api/app/* behind a secret cookie; the edge (player.caddy
# @authed) also accepts a valid HTTP Basic-auth header. Bake the base64 of "user:pass" here so the
# native app's prod tier can reach the gated API. Generate with:
#   printf '%s' 'marko:YOUR_PREVIEW_PASSWORD' | base64
# Empty => prod tier hits the coming-soon page (dev/laptop tier is unaffected).
#
# Expected to be tree-shaken from release builds (the tier switch is prod-locked there, so the
# const goes unused). That is the BUNDLER'S choice, not a guarantee we make: this is a build-time
# substitution, so the literal reaches the bundle and only disappears if the fold happens.
# `make mobile-build-release` greps the built artifact for it and FAILS rather than trusting that.
# It IS present in internal builds by design — treat an internal .ipa/.apk as carrying it.
VITE_PREVIEW_BASIC_AUTH=

# Coming-soon gate COOKIE value (native prod tier, internal builds only). Preferred over the Basic
# header above because it doesn't collide with a signed-in user's `Authorization: Bearer` — caddy's
# @preview_ok matches the cookie before @authed. The app seeds it into the native cookie jar at
# startup (services/native.ts :: initGateCookie). Get the value from the /preview handshake:
#   curl -s -i -u 'marko:YOUR_PREVIEW_PASSWORD' https://closelistening.app/preview | grep -i set-cookie
# then copy the cl_preview=<value> token (the <value> part only).
VITE_PREVIEW_COOKIE=

# --- Dev-tier on-device o11y ---
#
# A native WebView serves the app from an https origin and BLOCKS a plain-http telemetry target as
# mixed content, silently. So both dev targets must be https. The routes below are the ones that
# actually work, measured 2026-10-03 — the guesses that preceded them did not:
#
#   UMAMI: use the PUBLIC beacon host for BOTH tiers and let the WEBSITE ID choose the tier.
#     VITE_UMAMI_SRC=https://analytics.closelistening.app/script.js
#     VITE_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=<dev site id>      <- in .env.mobile
#     VITE_UMAMI_WEBSITE_ID=<prod site id>     <- in .env.mobile.testflight
#   It is the same Umami behind the tunnel and it routes purely by website id: a POST carrying the
#   DEV id returned a cache token whose decoded websiteId is the dev site, the row landed on the dev
#   site, and the prod site got zero. No tailnet needed, which also means it works on cellular.
#
#   Two tailnet routes were tried first and do NOT work, so don't re-derive them:
#     https://umami.<tailnet>.ts.net/script.js      -> does not resolve from every account (HTTP 000)
#     https://homelab.<tailnet>.ts.net/umami/script.js -> 404. Umami is a Next.js app emitting
#        root-absolute /_next assets, which cannot survive a stripped subpath. The homelab README
#        says exactly this, which is why Umami has its own cert node for the admin UI.
#
#   GLITCHTIP: the homelab reverse proxy DOES work for ingest (one endpoint, no asset paths):
#     VITE_SENTRY_DSN_PLAYER_DEV=https://<dashless-key>@homelab.<tailnet>.ts.net/glitchtip/<project>
#   Verified by POSTing to .../glitchtip/api/<project>/store/ -> 200, event stored in that project.
#   Use the DASHLESS key: the browser @sentry SDK rejects a dashed-UUID key with "Invalid Dsn" and
#   then drops every error silently, while GlitchTip's ingest accepts both — so a curl test passes
#   and the app still reports nothing.
#
# Point this at a DEV GlitchTip project, never the one the deployed app uses: a crash from a phone
# debug build in the prod project is indistinguishable from a real user's crash.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN_PLAYER_DEV=
# Legacy split, kept for the web dev server only. On device, set VITE_UMAMI_SRC above instead —
# `installUmami()` prefers VITE_UMAMI_SRC and falls back to this.
VITE_UMAMI_SRC_DEV=
